# quanta-memory-platform — Cursor Rules

## Cursor — Architecture Enforcement (read before every edit)

This repo is a **portable, host-independent** memory-platform workspace, not a
flat Rust project and not an application. Cursor must not paste code from the
Semantica monorepo (or any host) without re-homing it to the **allowed crate
graph** below. Violations fail clippy `-D warnings`, `cargo deny`, the doctor
gate, and the extraction pre-flight.

### Crate graph (only these workspace edges — strict `core <- facade <- testkit`)

```text
quanta-memory-platform-core    -> (none — pure hexagonal kernel)
quanta-memory-platform-facade  -> quanta-memory-platform-core
quanta-memory-platform-testkit -> quanta-memory-platform-core, quanta-memory-platform-facade
quanta-memory-host (Semantica) -> quanta-memory-platform-{core,facade}   # OUTSIDE this repo
```

**Hard bans:**

| Action | Why |
| --- | --- |
| `core` names `facade`/`testkit`, or `facade` names `testkit` | Inverts the direction; fails extraction pre-flight |
| Add a dependency from this repo back to Semantica / the host | Direction is host -> platform, never the reverse |
| `domain/` or `ports/` imports `crate::adapters` | Hexagonal direction is inward-only |
| `std::fs` / `std::env` / `SystemTime::now` in `domain/` or `ports/` | Kernel is pure; I/O & clocks belong behind ports/adapters |
| Semantica nouns (`Semantica*`, `quanta_runtime`, `codegraph`) in public API | Library must stay host-independent |
| Raw broker constructor in public code | Use the builder-first surface (`MemoryPlatformBuilder`, `ProcessMemoryBrokerBuilder`) |
| A path dependency escaping `crates/` | Breaks standalone extraction |
| Storage backing (redb) leaking outside its feature gate | Backings stay confined + feature-gated |

### Where new code goes

| You are implementing… | Put it in… | Do NOT put it in… |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Budget policy, presets, manifests, residency | `core/src/domain/policy.rs`, `config.rs` | `facade` |
| Accounting algebra (reserve/commit/release, invariants) | `core/src/domain/ledger.rs` | `api`, `facade` |
| Pressure / reclaim / scheduling | `core/src/application/{controller,scheduler}.rs` | `domain` |
| New capability trait (a port) | `core/src/ports/{pin,scratch,store,telemetry}.rs` | `domain`, `adapters` |
| Port implementation (infra) | `core/src/adapters/` | `domain`, `ports` |
| Public broker surface / builder | `core/src/api/{broker,builder,control}.rs` | `domain` |
| A portable store / pipeline (feature-sliced) | `facade/src/<feature>/service.rs` + builder in `shared/` | `core` |
| A storage backing (redb) | `facade/src/<feature>/backings/<name>.rs` (feature-gated) | service code |
| Conformance / fault / fixture / doctor tooling | `testkit/src/{conformance,faults,fixtures,doctor}/` | `core`, `facade` |

### Core kernel rules (common mistakes)

- **`domain/` stays pure** — no I/O, no clocks, no env, no `crate::adapters`.
- **One error type** — return `MemoryError` / `MemoryResult<T>`; never
  `map_err(|_| …)` (drops the source). Preserve `{e}` in the message.
- **Fail-closed before mutation** — validate the whole operation first; every
  mutating ledger op either fully succeeds or leaves the ledger untouched.
- **Telemetry is advisory** — allocator telemetry never mutates ledger counters.
- **Public API is the `lib.rs` re-exports** — keep internals `pub(crate)`; don't
  add raw constructor backdoors past the builder-first surface.

### Before submitting a Cursor diff

1. List every file you touched and which crate/layer owns it.
2. `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings`
3. `cargo check --workspace --all-features` and, if you added a feature path,
   build that feature alone.
4. `scripts/extract-to-standalone-repo.sh --dry-run /tmp/qmp-check` if you changed
   any `Cargo.toml` or cross-crate `use` path.
5. If you edited `tools/pm/sources/**`, run `python3 tools/pm/pm.py sync` then
   `python3 tools/pm/pm.py lint`.

## Rule Cheatsheet (self-check before the gate)

Every row is enforced fail-closed by `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets
--all-features -- -D warnings`, `cargo deny`, `scripts/regression-guards.sh`
(semgrep rules in `tools/semgrep/qmp-regressions.yml`, with a grep fallback), or
`scripts/memory-platform-doctor`. Fix the code, or add a scoped
`#[allow(<lint>)]` with a one-line `-- reason: <…>` on the same item (for a
semgrep row, a trailing `// nosemgrep: <rule-id> -- reason: <…>`).

| Don't write this | Write this instead | Enforced by |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `x.unwrap()` in non-test code | `x.ok_or_else(|| MemoryError::config("…"))?` | clippy `-D warnings`, review |
| `panic!` / `unreachable!` / `todo!` / `unimplemented!` in shipping paths | return a `MemoryError` variant | clippy, review |
| `.expect("")` / `.expect("TODO")` | only in `#[cfg(test)]`, with `.expect("<why this cannot fail>")` | review |
| `Err(_) => {}` / `let _ = fallible();` / `.ok();` | propagate with `?` or map to a typed `MemoryError` | review |
| `map_err(|_| …)` (drops the source) | preserve it: `map_err(|e| MemoryError::config(format!("…: {e}")))` | clippy `map_err_ignore`, review |
| bare `#[allow(<lint>)]` (no reason) | append `-- reason: <…>` on the same line | review |
| `arr[i]` / `&s[a..b]` on untrusted indices | `arr.get(i)` / checked slicing | clippy, review |
| `n as u64` (lossy / truncating) | `u64::try_from(n)?` or a checked/saturating op | clippy cast lints |
| `x as i64` / `as i32` / `as i16` / `as i8` (sign-flips a magnitude — the `bytes as i64` ledger-corruption bug) | keep `u64` magnitudes with saturating/checked ops; `i64::try_from(..)?`; widen a signed *diff* to `i128` | regression-guards (`qmp-magnitude-as-signed`) |
| `a.saturating_mul(b) / c` (saturates BEFORE the divide → under-reports the ratio: the pressure-percent / weighted-cap bug) | `u64::try_from(u128::from(a) * u128::from(b) / u128::from(c))` | regression-guards (`qmp-saturating-mul-then-divide`) |
| `Atomic::fetch_sub(..)` on a counter (can underflow → wraps to MAX: the spilled-count drift) | saturating CAS: `c.fetch_update(SeqCst, SeqCst, \|v\| Some(v.saturating_sub(n)))` | regression-guards (`qmp-atomic-fetch-sub`) |
| wrapping arithmetic on byte/budget counters | `checked_add` / `saturating_sub` per the documented invariant | review (ledger correctness) |
| live reconfigure / policy swap that re-checks only future reserves, not the CURRENT resident usage (e.g. raising `headroom_bytes` over live non-Headroom bytes) | re-validate the live snapshot against the NEW policy and fail-closed before installing it | review (ledger invariant 5) |
| byte-target sweep/reclaim loop that breaks only on `reclaimed >= target` | also handle zero-contribution entries / exhaustion so a positive target can't over-evict (skip zero-byte entries) | review |
| durable-queue FIFO key taken from an atomic counter OUTSIDE the write txn | derive the next key from the table INSIDE the write transaction (atomic, restart-safe, no skipped/duplicate keys) | review (spill backing) |
| `a == b` on floats | epsilon / ordered comparison | clippy `float_cmp` |
| `dbg!(...)` | remove it | clippy `dbg_macro` |
| `std::fs` / `std::env` / `SystemTime::now` / `Instant::now` in `domain/` or `ports/` | route through a port + adapter; keep domain pure | review, architecture rule |
| `crate::adapters` imported from `domain/` or `ports/` | depend on the port trait only | review (inward-only) |
| `facade`/`testkit` named from `core` (or `testkit` from `facade`) | keep `core <- facade <- testkit` | extraction pre-flight, review |
| Semantica nouns (`Semantica*`, `quanta_runtime`, `codegraph`) in public API | keep the library host-independent | doctor, review |
| raw broker constructor backdoor in public code | go through the builder-first surface | doctor (raw-constructor residue) |
| stale `V1`/`V2` / old renamed symbols | use the current names | doctor |
| allocator telemetry mutating ledger counters | telemetry is **advisory** — never writes counters | review (ledger invariant) |
| mutating the ledger partway then erroring | validate first; fail-closed BEFORE any mutation | review (all-or-nothing) |
| `assert!(true)` / `assert_eq!(x, x)` placeholder test | assert the real behavior / invariant | review |
| `#[ignore]` without a reason | `#[ignore = "<reason>"]` | review |

Clippy policy: `clippy.toml`. Run the full rail with `scripts/check-workspace.sh`.

## Architecture & Crate Boundaries

`quanta-memory-platform` is a portable, host-independent memory budgeting /
accounting workspace. It is consumed by the Semantica monorepo through a host
adapter (`quanta-memory-host`) that lives **outside this repo**, and it is
designed to be extracted into its own git repository with no rewrites. The crate
graph is the architecture authority.

Allowed workspace edges (strict, acyclic — `core <- facade <- testkit`):

```text
quanta-memory-platform-core    -> (none — pure)
quanta-memory-platform-facade  -> quanta-memory-platform-core
quanta-memory-platform-testkit -> quanta-memory-platform-core, quanta-memory-platform-facade
quanta-memory-host (Semantica) -> quanta-memory-platform-{core,facade}   # OUTSIDE this repo
```

No member depends upward. `core` must not name `facade`/`testkit`; `facade` must
not name `testkit`. The host adapter never appears as a dependency of this repo —
that would invert the direction.

Layout:

```text
crates/
  quanta-memory-platform-core/     # pure hexagonal kernel
    src/api/         # public broker surface: broker, builder, control
    src/application/ # use-case services: controller (pressure), scheduler
    src/domain/      # config, context, errors, ledger (accounting SSOT), policy, stats
    src/ports/       # trait definitions: pin, scratch, store, telemetry
    src/adapters/    # port impls: broker-backed scratch, hdrhistogram, metrics; explicit unbudgeted pin
  quanta-memory-platform-facade/   # feature-sliced portable stores + pipelines
    src/{payload_store,retained_map,revocable_store,transcript_store,publish_pipeline,spill}/
    src/{presets,shared}/
  quanta-memory-platform-testkit/  # conformance / chaos / alloc tooling (never a prod dep)
    src/{conformance,faults,fixtures,doctor,fake,alloc_tracking}/
```

Boundary rules (hexagonal direction is inward-only):

- **`domain/`** is pure: no I/O, no `std::fs`, no `std::env`, no wall-clock
  (`SystemTime::now` / `Instant::now`), and it must NOT import `crate::adapters`.
  It depends on its own types plus the `ports/` trait shapes.
- **`ports/`** holds trait definitions only — no concrete adapters, no I/O.
- **`adapters/`** implement port traits; infra types (hdrhistogram, metrics) live
  here. Allocator telemetry from adapters is **advisory**: it never mutates ledger
  counters. The in-memory pin registry is explicitly named `UnbudgetedPinRegistry`
  and exists only behind the non-default `unbudgeted_pin` feature.
- **`application/`** orchestrates domain + ports; carries no host nouns.
- **`api/`** is the public broker surface, builder-first. Low-level broker
  backdoors stay `pub(crate)` / unexported.
- **`facade`** composes core behind builders; production storage backings such
  as redb are feature-gated and confined to their `*/backings/` modules.
- **`testkit`** is NEVER a mandatory production dependency. `alloc_tracking` (the
  only `tracking-allocator` integration) is feature-gated so it can never enter a
  production graph.

Forbidden usages (fail-closed under `-D warnings` + repo-local doctor + deny):

- Semantica nouns in the public API (`Semantica*`, `quanta_runtime`, `codegraph`).
  The portable library carries none.
- `core` depending on `facade`/`testkit`; `facade` depending on `testkit`.
- A path dependency escaping the workspace (breaks extraction).
- `crate::adapters` imported from `domain/` or `ports/`.
- Raw constructor backdoors leaking past the builder-first surface; stale
  `V1`/`V2` / renamed-symbol naming (the doctor rail flags both).

Enforcement: `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings`,
`cargo deny --all-features check`, `scripts/memory-platform-doctor`, and the
extraction pre-flight in `scripts/extract-to-standalone-repo.sh` (member
presence, dependency direction, no escaping path deps, self-hosting file set,
and `cargo metadata` resolves). If a change needs a NEW crate edge, stop and
reconsider — do not add the import to make it compile.

## Build & Front Door

Plain `cargo` is the front door — there is no `just`/`nextest`/`uv` in this repo.
`scripts/check-workspace.sh` is the one-shot lint rail and includes the
per-feature matrix; `scripts/memory-platform-doctor` is the repo-local doctor
rail for fmt/clippy/feature-matrix/check/test/examples/deny. Closeout reports
cite the exact commands run.

Baseline commands:

```bash
cargo check --workspace --all-features
cargo build --examples --all-features
cargo fmt --all --check
cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings
cargo deny --all-features check
cargo test --workspace --all-features

# one-shot lint rail (fmt + clippy -D warnings + check + examples + deny):
scripts/check-workspace.sh

# repo-local doctor rail (fmt + clippy -D warnings + feature matrix + check + test + examples + deny):
scripts/memory-platform-doctor
```

The extracted repo is self-hosting: its own scripts do not depend on
`tools/ci/gates/*`. Semantica may still wrap this repo with additional external
gates after host-adapter rewires.

Examples (compile against the real public APIs):

```bash
cargo run -p quanta-memory-platform-facade --example hello_world
cargo run -p quanta-memory-platform-facade --example effective_budget_report
```

Agent docs:

```bash
python3 tools/pm/pm.py sync     # regenerate AGENTS.md / CLAUDE.md / .codex/* / .cursorrules
python3 tools/pm/pm.py lint     # fail on generated-doc drift
python3 tools/pm/pm.py status   # show targets + ok/drift/missing state
python3 tools/pm/tests          # via: python3 -m pytest tools/pm/tests -q
```

Config lives at the repo root: `clippy.toml` (`too-many-arguments-threshold = 8`,
`type-complexity-threshold = 300`), `rustfmt.toml` (edition 2021, `max_width = 100`,
`use_small_heuristics = "Max"`), `deny.toml` (license allow-list; private members
exempt), `.cargo/config.toml`. Toolchain: cargo 1.95.0.

Feature flags (the matrix runs clippy `-D warnings` on default, bare, each leaf,
`full`, and all-features; features must not leak through dependency defaults):

- **core:** `pressure`, `scheduler`, `live_control`, `unbudgeted_pin` (explicit experimental adapter),
  `telemetry` (= `metrics` + `hdrhistogram`), `full`
- **facade:** `core-live-accounting`, `payload_store`, `retained_map`, `revocable_store`,
  `transcript`, `publish_pipeline`, `spill` (`spill-redb` backing), `full`
- **testkit:** `fixtures`, `doctor`, `conformance`, `faults`,
  `alloc_tracking` (tracking-allocator), `full`

Facade disables core dependency defaults; only `core-live-accounting` may
activate core `live_control`. The feature matrix checks the resolved Cargo graph,
not only whether the extra symbols happen to compile.

Extraction into a standalone repo is mechanical and fail-closed:

```bash
scripts/extract-to-standalone-repo.sh --dry-run /path/to/dest   # pre-flight only
scripts/extract-to-standalone-repo.sh /path/to/dest             # copy + git init + verify
```

## Test Topology

Tests must **catch real regressions** in budget accounting, policy resolution,
pressure/scheduling, and the portable stores — not merely import modules.

Placement:

| If it proves… | Put it in… |
| --- | --- |
| Ledger reserve/commit/release + the 4 invariants | `core/src/domain/ledger.rs` `#[cfg(test)]` |
| Policy / manifest / preset / residency validation | `core/src/domain/{policy,config}.rs` `#[cfg(test)]` |
| Pressure controller / scheduler behavior | `core/src/application/{controller,scheduler}.rs` `#[cfg(test)]` |
| Public broker surface end-to-end (reserve→commit→snapshot→release→rebuild) | `core/src/api/control.rs` `#[cfg(test)]` |
| Cross-cutting policy contracts | `core/tests/wave1_policy_contracts.rs` |
| A facade store / pipeline behavior | that feature's `service.rs` `#[cfg(test)]` |
| A storage backing (redb) round-trip | the backing module under its feature |
| "the conformance suite actually bites" | `testkit` conformance + `self_tests` |
| Deterministic fault/chaos scenarios | `testkit/src/faults/` |

Conventions:

- **Conformance must bite.** The testkit's meta-requirement: feed a deliberately
  broken/lying fake into the public suite and assert it is REJECTED, alongside the
  honest-fake and real-broker positive cases. A suite that passes everything is
  worthless — preserve this when extending.
- **Assert invariants, not vibes.** After an op, assert the ledger invariants
  (`total_owner_bytes`, class/owner balance) hold — not just that a call returned
  `Ok`. No `assert!(true)` / `assert_eq!(x, x)` placeholders.
- **Assert on the `MemoryError` variant**, not on stringly-typed message
  substrings where avoidable.
- **Feature-gate tests** with the feature they exercise; `--all-features` must be
  green and each feature must build alone.
- `#[ignore]` needs a reason: `#[ignore = "<why>"]`.

Run before closeout:

```bash
cargo test --workspace --all-features
cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings
scripts/regression-guards.sh          # semgrep regression guards (in check-workspace + doctor too)
scripts/check-workspace.sh
python3 -m pytest tools/pm/tests -q   # if you touched tools/pm
```

Regression guards (`scripts/regression-guards.sh`, rules in
`tools/semgrep/qmp-regressions.yml`) pin bug classes this repo already hit —
magnitude-to-signed casts, saturate-before-divide ratios, and underflowing
`fetch_sub`. A flagged-but-safe use needs a trailing
`// nosemgrep: <rule-id> -- reason: <…>`.

## Error Handling

A memory-accounting platform that **drops failures quietly** corrupts the ledger.
Fail loudly and fail-closed.

The single error type is `MemoryError` (`core/src/domain/errors.rs`):

```text
MemoryError = Build | Config | Capacity | InvalidState | Report | Unsupported
MemoryResult<T> = Result<T, MemoryError>
```

Domain-intent aliases all resolve to `MemoryError`: `CapacityError`, `PinError`,
`PublishError`, `StoreWriteError`, `StoreDeleteError`. Construct via the helpers
(`MemoryError::config("…")`, `::capacity("…")`, `::build("…")`, `::report("…")`).

Principles:

- **No silent recovery.** Production code must not coerce a capacity / config /
  invalid-state failure into an empty default or a swallowed `Ok`.
- **Fail-closed before mutation.** An over-budget reserve, an invalid policy, or a
  write during rebuild returns the appropriate `MemoryError` BEFORE touching the
  ledger — never half-apply then error.
- **Preserve the source.** When wrapping a backing error (redb), include
  it: `format!("spill write failed: {e}")`. Never `map_err(|_| …)`.
- **Pick the right variant.** Over-budget → `Capacity`; bad manifest/policy →
  `Config`; builder misuse → `Build`; operation illegal in current state (e.g.
  write during rebuild) → `InvalidState`; snapshot/report failure → `Report`;
  not-wired capability → `Unsupported`.

Rust patterns:

| Avoid | Prefer |
| --- | --- |
| `.unwrap()` in non-test code | `?` with a `MemoryError` variant, or `.ok_or_else(...)` |
| `.expect("")` / `.expect("TODO")` | tests only, with `.expect("<invariant that cannot fail>")` |
| `Err(_) => {}` / `let _ = fallible();` | propagate with `?` or map to a typed error |
| `panic!()` / `todo!()` in shipping paths | return `MemoryError` with an explicit variant |
| `map_err(|_| MemoryError::…)` | `map_err(|e| MemoryError::…(format!("…: {e}")))` |
| lossy `as` / wrapping math on counters | `try_from` + `checked_*` / `saturating_*` |

`MemoryError` derives `Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq` and implements
`std::error::Error` + `Display` — assert on the variant in tests, not on string
substrings where avoidable.

## Cursor Notes (mandatory)

### Generated rules

- `.cursorrules` is **generated** from `tools/pm/sources/**`.
- **Never** hand-edit `.cursorrules`, `AGENTS.md`, or `.codex/*`.
- After changing sources: `python3 tools/pm/pm.py sync` then `python3 tools/pm/pm.py lint`.

### Architecture is not negotiable

Read **`cursor-architecture.md`** (included in this file) before every task.
Cursor frequently violates boundaries when porting code from the Semantica
monorepo — treat that section as a checklist, not background reading.

**Stop and re-plan** if your diff:

- Makes `core` name `facade`/`testkit`, or `facade` name `testkit` (inverts direction)
- Adds a dependency from this repo back to Semantica / the host adapter
- Adds `std::fs` / `std::env` / `SystemTime::now` to `domain/` or `ports/`
- Imports `crate::adapters` from `domain/` or `ports/`
- Introduces a Semantica noun (`Semantica*`, `quanta_runtime`, `codegraph`) into
  the public API
- Adds a raw broker constructor instead of going through the builder-first surface

### Scoped edits

| Task | Allowed touch set |
| --- | --- |
| Ledger / policy / config | `core/src/domain/**` + that module's tests |
| Pressure / scheduler | `core/src/application/**` + tests |
| New port / adapter | `core/src/ports/**` + `core/src/adapters/**` |
| Public broker / builder | `core/src/api/**` + examples |
| Facade store / pipeline | `facade/src/<feature>/**` (service + backing) |
| Conformance / chaos tooling | `testkit/src/**` |

Do **not** "drive-by" refactor unrelated crates.

### Clippy / gate

- The lint bar is `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --all-features
  -- -D warnings`. Fix violations; do not blanket-`allow` without a one-line
  `-- reason: <…>` on that item.
- `map_err(|_| …)` is **always wrong** — include `{e}` in the message.
- Closeout for Rust changes: `scripts/check-workspace.sh` must pass, and the
  extraction dry-run if you changed any `Cargo.toml` or cross-crate `use` path.

### Portability

- The library must stay host-independent and extractable. When unsure, run
  `scripts/extract-to-standalone-repo.sh --dry-run /tmp/qmp`.

